      "content": "I want to build an NES emulator from scratch in [C++/Rust/your preferred language]. This is a learning project to understand low-level system architecture and emulation.\n\nFor the first phase, I need help setting up the foundation and beginning the 6502 CPU implementation:\n\n1. PROJECT STRUCTURE:\n\n- Set up a clean project structure with appropriate directories (src, include, tests, roms, etc.)\n\n- Configure a build system (CMake/Cargo/etc.)\n\n- Set up a testing framework for CPU instruction verification\n\n2. INITIAL 6502 CPU CORE:\n\n- Create the CPU struct/class with registers (A, X, Y, SP, PC, status flags)\n\n- Implement the fetch-decode-execute cycle\n\n- Start with these essential opcodes first:\n\n* LDA (Load Accumulator): 0xA9 (immediate), 0xA5 (zero page), 0xAD (absolute)\n\n* STA (Store Accumulator): 0x85 (zero page), 0x8D (absolute)\n\n* LDX, LDY (Load X/Y registers)\n\n* Basic arithmetic: ADC, SBC\n\n* Transfers: TAX, TAY, TXA, TYA\n\n* Stack operations: PHA, PLA, PHP, PLP\n\n* JMP, JSR, RTS\n\n* Branch instructions: BEQ, BNE, BCS, BCC\n\n3. MEMORY SYSTEM:\n\n- Implement a simple memory bus (64KB address space)\n\n- Basic read/write functions\n\n- Placeholder for mapper implementation later\n\n4. TESTING SETUP:\n\n- Show me how to structure CPU tests\n\n- Provide a few example tests for the implemented opcodes\n\n- Explain how I'll integrate nestest.nes later\n\nPlease provide well-commented code with explanations of the 6502 architecture decisions. I want to understand WHY things work this way, not just copy code. Include cycle counting from the start since timing will matter later.\n\nStart with the project structure and CPU core skeleton, then we'll implement opcodes incrementally."
